P(A\\times P(B))|=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054949 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ff27f61-1c9a-545a-9185-30e18e60d035" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054949 #edb/ref 1074 "For any set $S,$ we have $|P(S)|={2}^{|S|}.$ Also, $|S\\times T|=|S|\\cdot |T|.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n|A|=2,\\,|B|=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n|P(B)|={2}^{|B|}={2}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(A\\times P(B))| & ={2}^{|A\\times P(B)|} \\\\  & ={2}^{|A|\\,\\cdot \\,P(B)} \\\\  & ={2}^{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054979 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054979 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1195" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054979 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054979 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $A$ is a set. If $|A|=5$, then $|P(A)|=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054979 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "609807a5-fc69-5b9b-8c4b-4d835e4fe8a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054979 #edb/ref 1074 "For any set $A,$ we have $|P(A)|={2}^{|A|}.$ \n\nIn this case, $|A|=5,$ so we have\n\n$$\n|P(A)|={2}^{|A|}={2}^{5}=32.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055571 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055571 #edb/ref 1061 153365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055571 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cccf59fe-5d64-567d-835c-def5b2fb5c93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055571 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055572 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055572 #edb/ref 1061 153366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055572 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "72c3927c-894d-55ae-8094-79014856a6ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055572 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055573 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055573 #edb/ref 1061 153367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055573 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba17ca05-c9c8-5cb0-9680-49c6db006781" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055573 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055574 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055574 #edb/ref 1061 153371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055574 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d9225952-f921-5ed9-a6b9-726af08be8b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055574 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055575 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055575 #edb/ref 1061 153372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055575 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5f5b95d2-66ba-5245-bd79-47140cb7833a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055575 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055576 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055576 #edb/ref 1061 153376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055576 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d77da74f-73f3-59f5-9290-13740989504d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055576 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055577 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055577 #edb/ref 1061 153379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055577 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3544c1c7-cd8e-5b1d-a95a-e07e977f3121" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055577 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055578 #edb/ref 1061 153382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055578 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3f3fd95b-66c4-5c77-81de-9a4ba5c39f8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055578 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056045 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose $S$ is a set. The **power set** of $S,$ denoted $\\mathcal{P}(S),$ is the set containing every subset of $S,$ *including* the empty set.\n\nFor example, let's find the power set of the set\n\n$$\nS = \\{ 1,2,3 \\}.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we list out all the subsets of $S{:}$\n\n- The subset containing no elements is $\\emptyset.$\n\n- The subsets containing $1$ element are $\\{ 1 \\},$ $\\{ 2 \\},$ and $\\{ 3 \\}.$\n\n- The subsets containing $2$ elements are $\\{ 1,2 \\},$  $\\{ 1,3 \\},$ and  $\\{ 2,3 \\}.$\n\n- The subset containing $3$ elements is $S$ itself: $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}.$\n\nNow, let's put all these subsets together as elements of a single set. We get\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{P}(S) = \\big\\{ \\emptyset, \\{1\\}, \\{2\\}, \\{3\\}, \\{ 1,2 \\}, \\{ 1,3 \\}, \\{ 2,3 \\}, \\{ 1,2,3 \\} \\big\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056045 #edb/ref 1065 8241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056045 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cbbf2155-81d5-52d9-b9c0-2416958838b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056045 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056046 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a finite set $S$ with cardinality $|S|.$ Then, the cardinality of $\\mathcal P(S)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left|\\mathcal{P}(S)\\right| = 2^{\\left| S \\right|}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, a finite set with cardinality $|S|$ has $2^{|S|}$ subsets.\n\nFor example, for the set $S = \\{ a,b,c \\},$ we have $|S| = 3,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n\\left|\\mathcal{P}(S)\\right| = 2^{\\left| S \\right|} = 2^3 = 8.\n$$\n\nThis tells us that $S$ has $8$ subsets, and consequently, $\\mathcal{P}(S)$ has $8$ elements. \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{P}(S) = \\big\\{\n\\underbrace{\\emptyset}_1,\n\\underbrace{ \\{a\\}}_2,\n\\underbrace{ \\{b\\}}_3,\n\\underbrace{ \\{c\\}}_4,\n\\underbrace{ \\{ a,b \\}}_5,\n\\underbrace{ \\{ a,c \\}}_6,\n\\underbrace{ \\{ b,c \\}}_7,\n\\underbrace{ \\{ a,b,c \\}}_8 \n\\big\\}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- It's clear from our rule that if $S$ is finite, then $\\mathcal P(S)$ is finite.\n\n- It can be shown that if $S$ is infinite, then $\\mathcal P(S)$ is also infinite.\n\nWe'll discuss power sets of infinite sets at the end of this lesson and in future lessons." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056046 #edb/ref 1065 8242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056046 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fa764524-a501-5804-b1a1-6523e739b234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056046 #edb/ref 1067 "The Cardinality of a Power Set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056060 #edb/ref 1064 "Another way to generate all the subsets of $S=\\{ 1,2,3 \\}$ is using a tree diagram. We start off with the empty set $\\{\\}$ and generate additional subsets of $S$ by systematically inserting or excluding elements of $S.$\n\nLet's go through the process.\n\n- Start with the empty set $\\{\\}$ at the root node of our tree. This is the parent node.\n\n- Draw two branches pointing away from the parent node. The tip of each arrow gives a child node. Label one branch 'No' and the other 'Yes'.\n\n![](images/a4/a4b17d8114cc48cdc858d036b839d766dcb60f3b400a096406479c9daf7afac0.png)\n\n- We'll now write down a subset of $S$ at each child node:\n\n- At the child node (tip of the arrow) whose branch is labeled 'No,' we write down the parent node's set.\n\n$$\n\\{\\}\n$$\n\n- At the child node whose branch is labeled 'Yes,' we create a new set by inserting the *first* element of $S$ into the parent node's set. This gives the following subset of $S{:}$\n\n$$\n\\{1\\}\n$$\n\nWe now have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/61/61c6c192df26a22cac5dddad6b2692a519da055730fe9738f3173cb38229cf8b.png)\n\nWe repeat the process, setting the subsets found in the previous step as the new parent nodes. \n\n- We start by considering the parent node $\\{\\}{:}$\n\n- At the child node whose branch is labeled 'No', we write down the parent node's set.\n\n$$\n\\{\\}\n$$\n\n- At the child node whose branch is labeled 'Yes,' we create a new set by inserting the *second* element of $S$ into the parent node's set. This gives the following subset of $S{:}$\n\n$$\n\\{2\\}\n$$\n\nWe now have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/6e/6e43b6022b5939b824995936d8ab910b7b3d2e821a6c96be63606245f6e60dbb.png)\n\n- Then, we consider the parent node $\\{1\\}{:}$\n\n- At the child node whose branch is labeled 'No', we write down the parent node's set.\n\n$$\n\\{1\\}\n$$\n\n- At the child node whose branch is labeled 'Yes,' we create a new set by inserting the *second* element of $S$ into the parent node's set. This gives the following subset of $S{:}$\n\n$$\n\\{1,2\\}\n$$\n\nWe now have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/6f/6f81c80039f14260bdfdb8d55a34820874fe518e076d2718629d4faf385e92d5.png)\n\n- Finally, we repeat the process for the new parent nodes $\\{\\}, \\{2\\}, \\{1\\},$ and $\\{1,2\\}.$ \n\nOur final tree diagram looks as follows.\n\n![](images/12/127ee1187ebf3a2cc498c88326285d5adf5d609cf3276885e8c9d525435133eb.png)\n\nThe final column lists all subsets of $S.$ \n\n$$\n\\emptyset = \\{\\},\\quad \\{1\\},\\quad \\{2\\},\\quad \\{3\\},\\quad \\{ 1,2 \\},\\quad \\{ 1,3 \\},\\quad \\{ 2,3 \\},\\quad \\{ 1,2,3 \\}\n$$\n\nFinally then, we can write down $\\mathcal P(S){:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{P}(S) = \\big\\{ \\emptyset, \\{1\\}, \\{2\\}, \\{3\\}, \\{ 1,2 \\}, \\{ 1,3 \\}, \\{ 2,3 \\}, \\{ 1,2,3 \\} \\big\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056060 #edb/ref 1065 8511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056060 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7a2897eb-b185-5619-bc49-f2ef12df345a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056060 #edb/ref 1067 "Using a Tree Diagram" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056061 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, our primary focus was on power sets of *finite* sets. The definition of the power set of an *infinite* set mirrors that of finite sets. Namely, it comprises all possible subsets.\n\nAs we already understand, for finite sets, the size of power sets grows exponentially based on the size of the underlying set: \n\n$$\n\\big| \\mathcal{P}(X) \\big| = 2^{|X|}\n$$\n\nWhen dealing with power sets of infinite sets, the situation is more intricate. \n\nFor example, let's consider $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ the set containing all points on the Cartesian plane. What are the elements of the power set $\\mathcal P(\\mathbb R^2)?$\n\nAny collection of points on the plane is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2.$ Such subsets can be visualized as black-and-white images on the plane, where points belonging to the subset are drawn in black, and points not belonging to the subset are represented in white.\n\nFor instance, each of the following images gives us a specific element of $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R}^2){:}$\n\n![](images/bb/bbdb49640183563e0cb137b3c57095edd8b81bceac4dcda1bf3460371df17930.png)\n\nIt follows that any text, be it any or all existing Math Academy lessons, those planned for the future, or any other possible text (finite or infinite), including those not yet written, are all contained within $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R}^2).$ \n\n![](images/c4/c47487cf1a7253bfafd5419cd824df30669a575d4b90f3fddc36566e920325bc.png)\n\nIt's somewhat extraordinary that such a vast and important set can be expressed using just the symbols $\\mathcal P(\\mathbb R^2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056061 #edb/ref 1065 8512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056061 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ca3f303c-7c55-5abe-945a-dfe8a4816aa8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056061 #edb/ref 1067 "Power Sets of Infinite Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371485 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105137" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371485 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371485 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are sets. If $|X|=2$ and $|Y|=6,$ then $|P(X)|+|P(Y)|=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "32b58d47-77da-59a3-a1da-88971d941837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371485 #edb/ref 1074 "For any set $S$, $|P(S)|={2}^{|S|}.$ \n\nIn this case, $|X|=2$ and $|Y|=6,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(X)|+|P(Y)| & ={2}^{|X|}+{2}^{|Y|} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{2}^{6} \\\\  & =4+64 \\\\  & =68.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371486 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371486 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371486 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371486 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371486 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371486 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371486 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371486 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371486 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7e2617a4-d46c-5b9f-91af-14610bdb6ac3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371487 #edb/ref 1080 "64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371487 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371487 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f87ce3ea-2e44-566e-9046-42997d83dec3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371488 #edb/ref 1080 "60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371488 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371488 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e79ece2-46b0-5248-ada9-8809e4f1dc84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371489 #edb/ref 1080 "68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371489 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371489 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d948b64d-82a1-5417-9ca3-74c3bb7db176" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371490 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371490 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371490 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e7c87382-1036-5603-a5d9-8e82172c1e8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371491 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371491 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371491 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f6f8d4c1-e0b3-5770-a66a-4dafee82cadd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371638 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371638 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1204" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371638 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371638 #edb/ref 1071 "If $Y=\\{0\\}$, then $P(Y)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371638 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7946c285-f988-5068-b516-3298112d9fdb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371638 #edb/ref 1074 "The power set of $Y,$ denoted $P(Y),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $Y,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nSo, let's list out the subsets of $Y:$\n\n- The subset with no elements is $∅.$\n\n- The subset with $1$ element is $Y$ itself: $\\{0\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y)=\\{∅,\\{0\\}\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371639 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371639 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371639 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371639 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371639 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371639 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371639 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371639 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186494838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371639 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a8af6849-bbbd-5257-abdf-809ae009ea35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372391 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372391 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182215" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372391 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372391 #edb/ref 1071 "If $|B|=2$ then $|P(P(P(B)))|=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372391 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "23139fd9-d255-51c7-b39b-28cddd3cf6e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372391 #edb/ref 1074 "For any set $S$, we have $|P(S)|={2}^{|S|}.$ \n\nIn our case, we have $|B|=2.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n|P(B)|={2}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(P(B))| & ={2}^{|P(B)|} \\\\  & ={2}^{4} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(P(P(B)))| & ={2}^{|P(P(B))|} \\\\  & ={2}^{16} \\\\  & =65\\,536.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372392 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372392 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372392 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372392 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372392 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372392 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372392 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372392 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372392 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8713ad7c-37d7-5134-a2cb-fa0ff4713712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372393 #edb/ref 1080 "65\\,536" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372393 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372393 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cd097735-fa55-589d-9594-34bdbd607a7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372394 #edb/ref 1080 "16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372394 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372394 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3a46b546-0817-517d-ae23-82a6eae47732" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372395 #edb/ref 1080 "1\\,024" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372395 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372395 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6d0e2f07-9331-5d38-9395-f8fc67c1c11e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372396 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372396 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372396 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6921ab35-c60f-5f43-9eba-0889e5f87645" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372397 #edb/ref 1080 "256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372397 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372397 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d28a898e-a62b-5210-bada-7d9a8db4dc32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372405 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372405 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182223" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372405 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372405 #edb/ref 1071 "If $Y=\\{1\\}$ and $Z=\\{m,n\\}$ then $|P(Z)\\times P(Y\\times Z)|=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372405 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a57e2ec-6280-5dcd-8f47-bb36c3489753" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372405 #edb/ref 1074 "For any set $S,$ we have $|P(S)|={2}^{|S|}.$ Also, $|S\\times T|=|S|\\cdot |T|.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n|Y|=1,\\,|Z|=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(Y\\times Z)| & ={2}^{|Y\\times Z|} \\\\  & ={2}^{|Y|\\,\\cdot \\,|Z|} \\\\  & ={2}^{1\\cdot 2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(Z)\\times P(Y\\times Z)| & =|P(Z)|\\cdot |P(Y\\times Z)| \\\\  & ={2}^{|Z|}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372406 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372406 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372406 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372406 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372406 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372406 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372406 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372406 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372406 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "00c64fa3-c4a7-55bf-986b-6474978ea29c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372407 #edb/ref 1080 "64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372407 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372407 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1fa2c4ff-0153-57ef-b2da-d7857daa6674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372408 #edb/ref 1080 "16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372408 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372408 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0738be5e-320c-53d1-b477-85f540ec0e84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372409 #edb/ref 1080 "128" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372409 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372409 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "213da24e-3ba3-56e1-8f70-a2a77cb72023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372410 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372410 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372410 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "66674441-1c75-53d9-86ed-276306c7f760" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372411 #edb/ref 1080 "256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372411 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372411 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8542c241-c995-5fef-bed2-cf1e26117a7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372419 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472107 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372419 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472108 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372419 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472109 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372419 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182242" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372419 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372419 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $P(P(∅))={∅,{∅}}$\n- **II.** $P(P({∅}))={∅,{∅}}$\n- **III.** $P(P({0}))={∅,{∅},{{0}},{∅,{0}}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372419 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6c0e2c1-4d3b-51cf-9630-3617d7834c7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372419 #edb/ref 1074 "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the power set of the set $∅$ is ${∅}.$ Therefore, $P(P(∅))={∅,{∅}}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that we are given the set ${∅}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{∅}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({∅}))={∅,{∅},{{∅}},{∅,{∅}}}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that we are given the set ${0}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{0}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({0}))={∅,{∅},{{0}},{∅,{0}}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372426 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472110 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372426 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472111 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372426 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472112 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372426 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372426 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372426 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $P(P(2))={∅,{2}}$\n- **II.** $P(P({2}))={∅,{∅},{{2}},{∅,{2}}}$\n- **III.** $P(P({{2}}))={∅,{{2}}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372426 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "afe01a46-709c-5c6f-b721-547dd77ac300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372426 #edb/ref 1074 "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $2$ is not a set. So, the notation $P(P(2))$ does not make sense.\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that we are given the set ${2}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{2}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({2}))={∅,{∅},{{2}},{∅,{2}}}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that we are given the set ${{2}}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{{2}}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({{2}}))={∅,{∅},{{{2}}},{∅,{{2}}}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379141 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472104 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186379141 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472105 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186379141 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472106 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186379141 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182241" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379141 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379141 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets are finite?\n- **I.** $P({Z,t,n})$\n- **II.** $P(Z)$\n- **III.** $P({{2}^{n}∣n\\in Z})$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186379141 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2d277777-ae06-5b12-9091-65c43e7bf489" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379141 #edb/ref 1074 "The power set of a finite set is finite, while the power set of an infinite set is infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Set I is finite since the underlying set is finite. Notice that \n\n$$\nP({Z,t,n})={∅,\\,{Z},\\,{t},\\,{n},\\,{Z,t},{Z,n},\\,{t,n},\\,{Z,t,n}}\n$$\n\nhas cardinality $8.$\n\n- Set II is infinite since the underlying set $Z$ is infinite.\n\n- Set III is infinite since the underlying set ${{2}^{n}∣n\\in Z}$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380132 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186380133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380132 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80959" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380132 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380132 #edb/ref 1071 "If $B=\\{\\{1\\},\\{2\\}\\},$ then $P(B)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380132 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5de41a0c-cbd2-54e7-9d6b-cea2337e1df2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380132 #edb/ref 1074 "The power set of $B,$ denoted $P(B),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $B,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nSo, let's list all possible subsets of $B$ using a tree diagram.\n\n![](images/10/1043ca38333d8e62100931c1676cba3dc85521134b60b4a5682142618c447cbd.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram, the subsets of $B$ are\n\n$$\n∅=\\{\\},\\,\\{\\{1\\}\\},\\,\\{\\{2\\}\\},\\,\\{\\{1\\},\\{2\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\nP(B)=\\{∅,\\{\\{1\\}\\},\\{\\{2\\}\\},\\{\\{1\\},\\{2\\}\\}\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380133 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380133 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380133 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380133 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380133 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380133 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380133 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380133 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186494902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380133 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "540f0f32-e64b-559d-8803-d14a4f38339f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383673 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186383674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383673 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383673 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383673 #edb/ref 1071 "If $|X|=0$ then $|P(P(P(X)))|=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383673 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "17503d54-27f5-530c-9f6d-5a30b7aa466b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383673 #edb/ref 1074 "For any set $S$, we have $|P(S)|={2}^{|S|}.$ \n\nIn our case, we have $|X|=0.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n|P(X)|={2}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(P(X)))| & ={2}^{|P(X))|} \\\\  & ={2}^{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(P(P(X))| & ={2}^{|P(P(X)|} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383674 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383674 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383674 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383674 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383674 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383679 #edb/ref 17592